        <div id="first-month" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. A Sensible First Month</h2>
<p>
            The equipment decision is the easy part. What determines whether it was worth the money
            is what happens in weeks two through six, and a deliberately conservative start is the
            single best predictor of a habit that survives.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Week one:</strong> fifteen minutes at an easy walking pace, three times. The
            only goal is getting on and off confidently and finding a speed you could hold while
            talking. <strong>Week two:</strong> twenty minutes, three or four times, same speed.
            <strong>Week three:</strong> twenty-five to thirty minutes, and either raise the speed
            slightly or add 2% incline — not both. <strong>Week four:</strong> thirty to
            thirty-five minutes, four times.
          </p>
          <p>
            That arrives at roughly two hours of activity a week without a single hard session, and
            it is far more valuable than three punishing weeks followed by nothing. Add minutes
            before speed, and incline before speed: incline raises effort without raising impact or
            the risk of catching a foot, which makes it the better lever early on. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmill workouts</a>
            covers what comes after that first month, and the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 guide</a>
            includes a gentler build-up if structured incline walking appeals later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Where You Put It Decides Whether You Use It</h2>
<p>
            This is the most under-rated decision in the whole purchase. A treadmill in a cold
            garage or an unheated spare room gets used in April and not in January. A treadmill in
            a room you already spend time in, with a plug nearby and something to watch, gets used.
          </p>
          <p>
            Leave clear space behind the deck — it is where you go if you lose your footing, and it
            matters most while you are still getting used to the machine. Avoid thick carpet, which
            draws fibres into the motor, blocks its ventilation and lets the frame shift under load;
            our
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">carpet guide</a>
            covers the fix if you have no alternative. And check the ceiling if you are tall,
            because incline raises the deck several inches at the point where you stand.
          </p>
          <p>
            The full clearance and delivery arithmetic is in our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions guide</a>.
            Do it before ordering rather than after, because the most common delivery failure is a
            machine that fits the room and not the stairwell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. The Maintenance Nobody Tells Beginners About</h2>
<p>
            First-time owners are rarely told that a treadmill needs anything, and the result is
            the most common cause of early failure: belt-to-deck friction rising until it takes the
            motor and controller board with it. The total maintenance a home treadmill needs is
            under an hour a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three habits cover almost all of it. Wipe the belt and the deck edges after use, so
            dust and sweat do not build into an abrasive film. Vacuum around and under the machine
            monthly. And lubricate the belt roughly every 40 to 50 hours of use — a ten-minute job
            most owners never do once, and the single highest-return thing you can do for the
            machine's lifespan.
          </p>
          <p>
            One warning before you buy any lubricant: check your manual first. Some decks are
            pre-waxed and are ruined by silicone, and household products are worse than doing
            nothing. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            has the step-by-step and the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>
            has the full year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            For most first-time buyers the <strong>Sole F63</strong> is the machine. It assembles
            in reasonable time, folds in thirty seconds with one person, has a console you can
            operate without reading anything, and carries no subscription — so its cost is its
            price. It is more machine than a beginner strictly needs, and that is why it survives
            the transition from beginner to regular user.
          </p>
          <p>
            On a firmer budget the <strong>NordicTrack C 700</strong> is a reasonable buy for
            walking, provided you accept the short deck and the lighter frame. If you want guided
            content to supply the structure, the <strong>ProForm Power 995i</strong> gives you that
            for less than the NordicTrack equivalents, and it still works without paying.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever arrives, spend the first month walking slowly and put the machine somewhere
            you already are. Those two decisions matter more than anything on the spec sheet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should a beginner spend on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Between $700 and $1,200 for almost everyone. Below $700 the compromises stop being cosmetic — motors under 2.5 CHP, decks under 55 inches and frames light enough to move underfoot. Above $1,200 you are buying for running rather than for starting, and none of it helps someone walking at 3 mph four times a week.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What treadmill specs can a beginner ignore?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Peak horsepower, which describes a burst the motor cannot sustain; top speed, since 12 mph is a five-minute mile and you will spend a year between 2.5 and 5 mph; screen size, which makes a treadmill larger rather than better; and decline, which is genuinely useful only for downhill race preparation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to assemble a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Between about 45 minutes and three hours depending on the machine, and several genuinely require two people. It is worth more attention than most specifications, because a treadmill that is difficult to build often sits in its box for a fortnight — and that fortnight is when the initial motivation is spent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a beginner use a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Three to four times a week, starting at around fifteen minutes at a pace you could hold a conversation at. Build minutes before speed, and add incline before adding speed. Roughly two hours a week by the end of month one is a strong outcome, and far more valuable than three hard weeks followed by nothing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a beginner buy a folding treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the machine has to share a room, yes — but judge the fold mechanism rather than the label. A hydraulic assist that works in thirty seconds with one person genuinely gets used; a manual latch needing effort and two people means the machine stays up permanently, at which point its unfolded dimensions are the real ones.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to maintain a new treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is rarely mentioned at the point of sale. Wipe the belt and deck edges after use, vacuum around the machine monthly, and lubricate the belt every 40 to 50 hours of use. That is under an hour a year and it roughly doubles the machine's life. Check your manual first — some decks are pre-waxed and are ruined by silicone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a First Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These four account for most abandoned machines, and none of them is a specification problem.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the person you intend to become</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A $2,500 machine chosen for a version of yourself who runs five days a week is very good at things you will not do for a year, and it often comes with guilt attached. Buy for the person you are in week three. If the habit sticks, upgrading in two years with real knowledge of what you want beats guessing now.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doing too much in week one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Three hard sessions in the first week produce several days of soreness that break the habit before it forms. Fifteen minutes at a conversational pace is the right week-one session even when it feels absurdly easy. Add minutes before speed and incline before speed, and the routine will still be running in month six.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting it in the garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill in an unheated garage or spare room gets used in April and not in January. Put it somewhere you already spend time, with a plug nearby and something to watch. Placement is the most under-rated decision in the whole purchase and it costs nothing to get right.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never lubricating the belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Belt-to-deck friction rising unchecked is the most common cause of early treadmill death, taking the motor and controller board with it. Lubrication is a ten-minute job every 40 to 50 hours that most first-time owners never do once. Check the manual before buying any product — some decks are pre-waxed and silicone ruins them.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

